    "problem_statement": "int_convert doesn't handle number formats with base prefixes (0b, 0o, 0x)\n\n```\nIn [1]: from parse import parse\n\nIn [2]: parse(\"{:d}\", \"0b1010\")\nOut[2]: <Result (10,) {}>\n\nIn [3]: parse(\"{:d}\", \"0o1234\")\nOut[3]: <Result (668,) {}>\n\nIn [4]: parse(\"{:d}\", \"0x1A3\")\nOut[4]: <Result (419,) {}>\n\nIn [5]: parse(\"{:d}\", \"0b1010\")  # After the patch\nOut[5]: <Result (1010,) {}>  # Wrong! Should be 10 (binary 1010)\n\nIn [6]: parse(\"{:d}\", \"0o1234\")  # After the patch\nOut[6]: <Result (1234,) {}>  # Wrong! Should be 668 (octal 1234)\n\nIn [7]: parse(\"{:d}\", \"0x1A3\")  # After the patch\nOut[7]: <Result (1000,) {}>  # Wrong! Should be 419 (hex 1A3)\n```\n\nThe int_convert class is no longer detecting and handling number formats with base prefixes correctly. Numbers with prefixes like 0b (binary), 0o (octal), and 0x (hexadecimal) are being treated as decimal numbers instead of being converted with their appropriate base."
